WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.840
The Big Bang Theory is one of the most popular&nbsp;
sitcoms of all time, but the show isn't perfect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.440
Over the course of nearly 300 episodes,&nbsp;
writers sometimes forgot plot points and actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.920
occasionally lost touch with their characters.&nbsp;
Here are the goofs that are hardest to overlook.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.480
Sheldon Cooper is a creature of&nbsp;
habit. Perhaps because he is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.640
scientist striving to find meaning&nbsp;
and order in a chaotic universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:30.080
he exerts control over nearly every part of his&nbsp;
life. This manifests in his strict dinner schedule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.400
and his "spot." It's well understood among&nbsp;
his social circle that no one is to sit on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.800
the far right end of the brown couch in the&nbsp;
apartment's living room, except Sheldon.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:39.520
"Hello, Penny."

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:40.480
"Hello, Sheldon."

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:41.680
"You're in my spot."

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:46.480
He has called that comfortable and familiar&nbsp;
spot a, quote, "single point of consistency"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:52.160
in a world in flux, upon which he has placed&nbsp;
"eternal dibs," in part because it has a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:57.360
angle of the TV and never gets too hot or&nbsp;
too cold. On the prequel show Young Sheldon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.760
young Sheldon has a similar spot of&nbsp;
preference in his school cafeteria.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.000
Nevertheless, The Big Bang Theory's directors&nbsp;
sometimes forget about this major element of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.520
Sheldon's character, and allow other&nbsp;
people to sit in the hallowed spot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:14.080
even while Sheldon is in the room. It's weird&nbsp;
that someone so adamant about his seat on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:19.600
couch doesn't notice or mind when Howard or&nbsp;
Leonard occupies it, but hey — that's TV for you.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.400
Sheldon Cooper's strict approach to the&nbsp;
calendar applies to everything from meal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.400
planning to his leisure activities. Early in&nbsp;
the show, it's established that Sheldon and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.960
the guys have a weekly standing date to play&nbsp;
the video game Halo every Wednesday night.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.280
Penny hits Sheldon and Leonard's apartment&nbsp;
during one session and mentions that Christy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.720
her friend from back home in Nebraska,&nbsp;
showed up uninvited and is staying at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.000
her apartment. Upon hearing that Christy is&nbsp;
promiscuous, Howard flees the game and goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.720
across the hall to proposition her, and the&nbsp;
two wind up using Penny's bedroom to hook up.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.840
"Well, um, Howard and Christy are&nbsp;
kind of hooking up in my bedroom."

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.160
Penny, with nowhere to sleep that night, crashes&nbsp;
on the couch in Sheldon and Leonard's place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.360
That's all well and good until&nbsp;
the next morning, when a robe-clad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.240
Sheldon is mad that a sleeping Penny has&nbsp;
interrupted his Saturday morning tradition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:12.480
of watching Doctor Who. Either The Big Bang&nbsp;
Theory writers goofed up and forgot that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.840
previous evening was Wednesday, or Penny slept&nbsp;
continuously from Halo Night to Saturday morning.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:24.080
Roommate disputes are voluminous and varied on&nbsp;
The Big Bang Theory, especially between precise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:29.440
Sheldon and exasperated Leonard. As a preemptive&nbsp;
measure — and to make sure his home life strictly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.720
adheres to his tastes in every way — Sheldon draws&nbsp;
up a lengthy and elaborate "Roommate Agreement."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:40.240
Its existence is referred to often, as are its&nbsp;
comically numerous articles, riders, and clauses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:46.400
One aspect of the agreement, a "girlfriend clause"&nbsp;
is especially ridiculous. Per Sheldon's legalese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.880
any relevant woman is considered to be&nbsp;
cohabiting with Leonard and Sheldon and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.240
is subject to all the rules and costs of such&nbsp;
if she stays over for 10 straight nights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.280
more than nine total nights over three weeks, plus&nbsp;
all the weekends of a month, plus three weekdays.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.040
Even though this Roommate Agreement gets&nbsp;
brought up throughout The Big Bang Theory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.960
Leonard violates the "girlfriend clause"&nbsp;
with regularity and there's little to no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:13.600
fallout. All of Leonard's female romantic&nbsp;
partners stay over, and stay over often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:17.920
including Stephanie, Priya, and&nbsp;
Penny, with little incident.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.680
For the first few seasons of The Big&nbsp;
Bang Theory, Sheldon Cooper loves cats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.640
but in a strictly theoretical sense, as he's&nbsp;
allergic. Several times, Sheldon insists that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.360
Penny sing his favorite childhood song in order&nbsp;
to soothe and relax him in times of physical or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.480
emotional duress: "Soft Kitty," which is about&nbsp;
how a kitty is soft, warm, covered in fur, happy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.520
sleepy, and likes to purr. In The Fuzzy Boots&nbsp;
Corollary, the third episode of the entire show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:46.000
Leonard considers compensating for the lack of&nbsp;
a romantic relationship in his life by getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.560
a cat, assuring an extremely worried Sheldon&nbsp;
that the latter's medical sensitivities won't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:55.360
come in to play, because he's going to buy&nbsp;
a genetically-modified hypoallergenic cat.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:58.240
"Oh, I hope that scratching post is for you."

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.560
But three years later, in the fourth&nbsp;
season episode The Zazzy Substitution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.040
it's Sheldon who gets a cat to deal with the&nbsp;
loneliness he's enduring after breaking off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:12.880
his romance with Amy Farrah-Fowler. Worried that&nbsp;
the first cat, named Dr. J. Robert Oppenheimer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:18.080
after the famous scientist, is lonely, Sheldon&nbsp;
adopts more cats, all of them named after great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:23.680
men of science, all except for Zazzles, who is a&nbsp;
decidedly "zazzy" little fellow. Bringing home so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:27.920
many cats is a sign of an emotional breakdown&nbsp;
for Sheldon, but it doesn't trigger a physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:32.880
one — he doesn't exhibit a single symptom&nbsp;
of his previously explained cat allergy.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.600
"Now if you'll excuse me, the father of&nbsp;
the atomic bomb wants a saucer of milk."

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.560
While Howard Wolowitz is often mocked by&nbsp;
Sheldon Cooper for not having a PhD — he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:47.120
holds a mere master's degree in aerospace&nbsp;
engineering from MIT — he achieves something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.080
that no other character on the show does: He&nbsp;
goes to space. In a plot arc spanning seasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.240
five and six of the show, Howard is selected&nbsp;
to travel to the International Space Station&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.400
alongside two other astronauts. Heading to&nbsp;
space has its ups and downs for the goofy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.560
Howard, but it's ultimately a rewarding&nbsp;
experience he appreciates — which is good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:08.480
because in real life, someone like him&nbsp;
would likely never make it to the ISS.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:13.760
In a real-life space training program, he would&nbsp;
have been weeded out and disqualified in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:18.480
number of ways, such as when he has trouble&nbsp;
navigating NASA 's flight-preparation system.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.720
"They do it over and over again, no&nbsp;
matter how many times you throw up."

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:26.160
Neither would he pass the physical,&nbsp;
what with his genetic predisposition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.000
to heart problems and severe asthma&nbsp;
both putting him out of the running.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:35.760
Sitcoms are decidedly unrealistic, and The&nbsp;
Big Bang Theory is no exception. It seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.640
unlikely that a bunch of thoughtful, intelligent&nbsp;
people with active social lives would hang around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:45.360
someone as mean, controlling, and judgmental&nbsp;
as Sheldon Cooper for more than a dozen years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.440
But it's downright illogical that someone&nbsp;
like Penny would live next door to Sheldon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.680
and Leonard in the first place. Seriously,&nbsp;
the entire premise of the show – guy falls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.040
in love with attractive neighbor&nbsp;
— is built on an economic fallacy.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.520
When the show begins in 2007, Sheldon and&nbsp;
Leonard have been roommates for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:06.480
both contributing to the rent on their spacious&nbsp;
Pasadena apartment with the salaries they earn as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.640
doctorate-holding, high-level scientific&nbsp;
researchers at the prestigious California&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:16.080
Institute of Technology. According to Forbes, each&nbsp;
character would be pulling in at least $100,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:21.040
a year, more than enough to afford half of&nbsp;
an apartment in expensive Pasadena. And yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:24.160
Penny moves into the same building,&nbsp;
right across the hall. Granted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:28.000
her digs feature just one bedroom, so it&nbsp;
would cost a little less than the guys' pad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.160
but it doesn't seem possible that she'd be&nbsp;
able to afford similar accommodations as two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.800
well-compensated scientists on the stingy&nbsp;
salary of a Cheesecake Factory waitress.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.240
Sheldon Cooper isn't like most people. A&nbsp;
super-genius who attended high school as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.960
a little boy and graduated college by the time&nbsp;
he was 14, he's always had a hard time relating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:52.160
to people his own age. Moreover, he operates&nbsp;
from such a staunchly logical and scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:57.840
mindset that he fails to grasp emotionally-driven&nbsp;
behaviors or subtle nuances in conversations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:03.200
particularly sarcasm. When he senses that someone&nbsp;
is being sarcastic, he has to receive confirmation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:08.400
of it – Leonard even resorts to holding up a sign&nbsp;
that reads "sarcasm" when its use is appropriate.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:09.120
"What's funny?"

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:11.520
"That wasn't sarcasm?"

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.840
Despite Sheldon's complex relationship with&nbsp;
sarcasm, however, he's still a character in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:21.120
broad sitcom, and sitcom characters are the most&nbsp;
naturally sarcastic people in all of fiction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:26.560
Within that segment, Sheldon Cooper is arguably&nbsp;
one of the most acerbic, sneering, and, yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:31.280
sarcastic individuals to ever grace a TV&nbsp;
screen. He doesn't understand sarcasm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:33.280
apparently, but he employs it all the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:37.760
This one is such a glaring discrepancy that&nbsp;
it's really just better to let it go entirely.

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:40.160
"You know how you're always&nbsp;
trying to learn about sarcasm?"

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:40.320
"No."

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:41.120
"No?"

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:42.720
"I was being sarcastic."

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:48.000
While all four male main characters and their&nbsp;
partners on The Big Bang Theory gather most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.160
nights for takeout dinner at the big apartment,&nbsp;
the guys all work together at the California&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:56.640
Institute of Technology, and as such also&nbsp;
get together seemingly every workday for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.320
lunch in the school's cafeteria. This&nbsp;
means there's a lot of eating on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:05.600
show – and pretending to consume food on stage&nbsp;
or screen is an acting challenge unto itself.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:09.920
The performers don't actually eat the various&nbsp;
sandwiches and salads on offer in the CalTech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:14.080
lunchroom. Instead, the actors just move&nbsp;
their forks and knives around to mimic the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:18.480
process of dining, without placing the prop&nbsp;
food into their mouths. They're remarkably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:22.560
bad at pulling this off convincingly,&nbsp;
and once you see it, it's hard to miss.

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:27.280
There exists a small pantheon of&nbsp;
heard-but-not-seen characters from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:32.160
across TV history, and Howard's mother,&nbsp;
Mrs. Wolowitz, is a prime example. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:37.760
what amounts to a running gag, the show strongly&nbsp;
establishes that Mrs. Wolowitz is morbidly obese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:41.600
so large, in fact, that no actress&nbsp;
could play her. But every so often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:46.000
an episode gives viewers more of Mrs.&nbsp;
Wolowitz than just her screaming at her son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:51.120
in an exaggerated New Jersey accent — and it&nbsp;
always conflicts with her allegedly huge size.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:54.880
At one point, she wraps her arms around&nbsp;
Raj, and while they're the only part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:58.720
of Mrs. Wolowitz on camera, they are&nbsp;
the limbs of an average-sized woman.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:08:59.920
"Where ya going?"

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:05.280
When Howard marries Bernadette in a&nbsp;
rooftop ceremony before he goes into space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:08.320
the mother of the groom is seen&nbsp;
in an overhead shot of the affair.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:13.360
Standing off to the side in a pink dress, Mrs.&nbsp;
Wolowitz doesn't seem to be all that heavyset.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:19.600
Perhaps it's because an occasional but major&nbsp;
role on the most popular sitcom on TV pays well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:24.240
Perhaps it's because celebrity stunt casting&nbsp;
is fun for a show's cast, crew, and fans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:28.400
Whatever the reason, The Big Bang Theory had&nbsp;
lots of fun casting well-known performers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:33.040
to play the parents of its regular characters.&nbsp;
The Good Wife and The Good Fight star Christine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:38.160
Baranski appeared on The Big Bang Theory 16&nbsp;
times as Leonard's mother, Beverly Hofstadter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:44.000
a world-renowned psychiatrist and neuroscientist.&nbsp;
The first time she shows up, in the 2009 episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:49.040
The Maternal Capacitance, she analyzes Penny&nbsp;
and helps her work through her parental issues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:53.440
At one point, Penny tells Beverly about her&nbsp;
childhood, referring to her father as "Bob."

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:57.680
"I mean, my mom could have just said,&nbsp;
'Bob! Get over it. She's a girl. Move on."

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:01.520
A year and a half later, Oscar-winner&nbsp;
Keith Carradine made his first of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:04.000
five appearances on The Big&nbsp;
Bang Theory as Penny's stern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:08.400
protective father. But he introduces&nbsp;
himself not as Bob, but as Wyatt.

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:09.520
"Thanks for the steak, Wyatt."

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:12.640
Penny's father apparently has&nbsp;
two first names? Regardless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:14.960
like Penny, his last name is never revealed.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:19.920
The Big Bang Theory is a show about&nbsp;
scientists, but not so much a show about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:24.320
science. While it's understood that most of the&nbsp;
main characters work to unlock the secrets of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:28.640
the universe in some way or another, the show&nbsp;
doesn't bog itself down with anything more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:33.440
the occasional mention of a famous scientist.&nbsp;
As a result, The Big Bang Theory rarely shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:37.520
the guys doing any real science work. When&nbsp;
they conduct experiments, they're fun things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:42.560
that make for good television, despite being&nbsp;
completely and woefully scientifically inaccurate.

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:47.760
In The Vengeance Formulation, co-worker&nbsp;
Barry Kripke pranks Sheldon before an NPR&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:51.680
interview by pumping his office&nbsp;
full of helium. The intended effect:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:54.160
Sheldon's voice goes hilariously high-pitched.

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:58.160
"As I was saying, an ordinary&nbsp;
magnet has two poles."

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:01.920
The real-life effect: Sheldon would have&nbsp;
suffocated to death with so much helium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:07.200
replacing life-giving oxygen. As for Leonard, one&nbsp;
of his job perks is getting to use a giant laser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:12.160
for science stuff. A real university's laser&nbsp;
would have numerous safety protocols in place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:16.000
such as the device not functioning if&nbsp;
the door to the laser room was open.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.800
And yet when Leonard uses his laser, he carelessly&nbsp;
leaves the door ajar, and he wears no safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:24.960
equipment while operating the laser. 
 
